<div class="header-banner">
    <h1>Lesson Planner – Theme # Wellness and Mindfulness – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<div class="table-container">
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="height: 50px;">
            <td style="width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="height: 50px;">
            <td style="width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="width: 37.5%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Wellness and Mindfulness</td>
            <td style="width: 37.5%;"><b>Theme:</b> How Exercise Helps My Body</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="height: 50px;">
            <td style="width: 25%;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose a personal diary entry. Interactive: Comment on peers' writing in various formats, including on paper and online.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a diary entry about personal wellness activities, including details about mindfulness and exercise habits and their impact on daily tasks (e.g., "Every morning, I stretch and meditate for five minutes before school. It helps me stay focused and calm during lessons." "After soccer practice, I take time to reflect on my progress, which helps me feel more motivated.")
                Can comment on a peer's wellness routine entry (e.g., "I think you can add riding a bike to your routines.")
                Can give feedback on a peer's mindfulness routine description (e.g., "I loved your tips for mindfulness. Maybe you can add a note on how mindfulness helps with stress.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 class="aoa-title">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<div class="table-container">
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</td>
                <td>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by asking students to individually write down 3-5 words or short phrases that describe "How I feel after exercising" or "My favorite exercise activity." Students share one word/phrase with a partner to activate prior knowledge about the theme "How Exercise Helps My Body" and prepare for writing.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a simple diary-like sentence on the board using target vocabulary. For example: "Today I felt a great <b>energy boost</b> after my morning <b>exercise</b>." The teacher models the pronunciation of key words like "energy," "exercise," and "mindfulness," focusing on stress in multisyllabic words and demonstrating minimal pairs if they naturally arise (e.g., "could" vs. "cooed" in a sentence context if referring to possibilities related to wellness). For example, "You *could* feel more <b>relaxed</b> if you practiced *cooing* (not relevant to topic, but for vowel contrast) during meditation." (Teacher needs to be careful with minimal pairs not directly related to content. Better to stick to stress and longer words). The teacher will focus on stress in words like "flexible," "resilience," and "mindfulness" while writing and articulating them.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the task and vocabulary.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a diary entry a formal letter to the principal or a personal record of your thoughts and feelings?" (Expected answer: Personal record)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a 'wellness routine' happen once a year, or regularly, like a daily habit?" (Expected answer: Regularly/Daily habit)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness"</td>
                <td>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, authentic-looking diary entry or blog post excerpt (approximately 60-80 words) written by a teenager describing their personal wellness routine, including exercise and mindfulness habits. The text should incorporate present simple for routines, present continuous for ongoing actions, collocations like "energy boost," and target vocabulary such as <b>mindfulness</b>, <b>exercise</b>, <b>benefits</b>, <b>flexibility</b>, <b>focus</b>, <b>relax</b>, <b>strengthen</b>, <b>mental well-being</b>, and <b>calm</b>. The register is informal and personal.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting texts of varying complexities to obtain general and specific information and critically analyze their content, specifically focusing on how the writer uses language to describe personal wellness activities.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing focus):</b> Students read the text and perform the following writing-based tasks:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> Write one sentence summarizing the main idea of the diary entry. (e.g., "The entry describes a teenager's daily routine for maintaining their health.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching/Identification:</b> Students read the text again and individually write down all the sentences that describe a specific <b>exercise</b> activity and sentences that describe a <b>mindfulness practice</b>. They also identify and list any collocations with health-related verbs they find (e.g., "boost my <b>energy</b>," "<b>improve</b> my <b>focus</b>").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Analysis for Writing:</b> Students identify and underline examples of the present simple (for habits/routines) and present continuous (for ongoing actions) used in the text. They then make a short note next to each, explaining why that tense was used.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Writing</td>
                <td>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences and short paragraphs using present simple for general statements and routines, present continuous for ongoing actions, collocations with health-related verbs, and target vocabulary related to wellness and mindfulness.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ol>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks related to wellness activities. Students complete the sentences by choosing the correct target vocabulary word from a provided box (e.g., "Daily ____ helps to ____ my <b>mental well-being</b>." Options: 'meditation'/'boost'). This activity reinforces vocabulary and collocations. For example: "I often feel a great <b>energy boost</b> after my morning <b>exercise</b> routine." "Practicing <b>mindfulness</b> helps to <b>improve</b> my <b>focus</b>."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Expansion/Transformation:</b> Students are given simple sentences about wellness. They rewrite and expand each sentence by adding an adjective or an adverb, or by transforming it to use either the present simple or present continuous, and incorporating at least one new target vocabulary word. For instance:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I stretch." → "I stretch *flexibly* every morning, which <b>promotes</b> my overall <b>health</b>."</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I meditate now." → "I <b>am practicing mindfulness</b> right now to feel <b>calm</b> and <b>peaceful</b>."</li>
                            </ul>
                            The teacher guides them to ensure grammatical accuracy and vocabulary use.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Sentence Check (Writing):</b> Students individually write 3-4 sentences describing their own (or an imagined) wellness habits, ensuring they use both present simple and present continuous correctly, along with at least three target vocabulary words. They then exchange their written sentences with a partner. Each partner reads the other's sentences and writes a brief, constructive comment or a question (e.g., "Good use of 'boost'! How does it <b>improve</b> your <b>focus</b>?", "Your sentence about <b>flexibility</b> is clear!"). This addresses the sociolinguistic competence of engaging in discussions by giving written feedback.</li>
                    </ol>
                </td>
                <td></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Wellness and Mindfulness"</td>
                <td>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will compose a personal diary entry about their wellness and mindfulness routine and then provide written comments on a peer's entry.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written product (diary entry) followed by a peer feedback writing activity, on paper or a shared online document (e.g., Google Docs).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students will write about "How Exercise Helps My Body" by detailing their personal or imagined wellness routine within the "Wellness and Mindfulness" scenario.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in writing, accurate application of grammar (present simple, present continuous) and target vocabulary, collaborative interaction through written feedback, and communicative task completion.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Individual Writing:</b> Students write a diary entry (approximately 50-70 words) describing their own (or an imagined) wellness routine. They must include details about at least one exercise habit and one mindfulness practice, explaining how these activities impact their daily tasks, <b>mental well-being</b>, and physical <b>health</b>. They are required to use at least five target vocabulary words (e.g., <b>boost</b>, <b>focus</b>, <b>energized</b>, <b>relax</b>, <b>strength</b>, <b>resilience</b>) and demonstrate correct use of both present simple and present continuous. Pragmatic competence of "Sharing wellness practices" is embedded here.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Commenting (Written Interaction):</b> After writing their entries, students exchange their diary entries with a partner. Each student reads their peer's entry and writes 1-2 constructive comments or suggestions on the entry. Examples: "I think you can add riding a bike to your routines to <b>improve flexibility</b>," "I loved your tips for <b>mindfulness</b>. Maybe you can add a note on how <b>mindfulness</b> helps with stress to <b>enhance</b> the <b>benefits</b> you mentioned." This directly addresses the learning outcome for interactive writing and the sociolinguistic competence of contributing suggestions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</td>
                <td>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during writing and peer feedback, combined with a simple rubric for the diary entry and peer comments.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will submit their personal diary entry along with the written peer feedback they received. The teacher will review both to assess individual writing skills and the ability to provide constructive feedback.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content (Diary Entry):</b> Addresses wellness/mindfulness activities and their impact on daily tasks. (Achieved, Partially Achieved, Not Achieved)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (Diary Entry):</b> Correct use of present simple and present continuous. (Achieved, Partially Achieved, Not Achieved)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary (Diary Entry):</b> Uses at least 5 target vocabulary words effectively. (Achieved, Partially Achieved, Not Achieved)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Coherence & Clarity (Diary Entry):</b> Ideas are organized and easy to understand. (Achieved, Partially Achieved, Not Achieved)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Comments:</b> Provides 1-2 constructive, polite, and relevant comments/suggestions. (Achieved, Partially Achieved, Not Achieved)</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers on a small slip of paper.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase related to wellness that you used effectively in your diary entry today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about writing about your wellness routine, and what helped you overcome it?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></td>
                <td>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (Journaling or verbal sharing):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing about your wellness and exercise habits help you think more deeply about your own personal <b>routine</b>?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn from giving and receiving feedback on your diary entry regarding promoting <b>mental</b> and <b>physical well-being</b>?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which aspects of the writing task (composing the entry, or peer commenting) did students find most engaging? Were the grammar points (present simple/continuous) and target vocabulary adequately applied in their independent writing?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students struggling with structuring their writing or providing effective feedback? How can the pronunciation focus (stress on multisyllabic words like "flexibility," "resilience," "mindfulness") be reinforced in a writing-focused lesson, perhaps by having students read their entries aloud and focusing on clarity?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate the information from their individual wellness routines and the learned vocabulary to create a collaborative "Wellness Guide" or a short presentation. This will require them to mediate and integrate information from their own and peers' written work, building directly on the writing skills practiced today.</p>
                </td>
                <td></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>